A||N||8||kornholio||Help needed from Fiero voltage experts!||||5
Z||000000||kornholio||10-06-1999||06:42 PM||||I got a new alternator and I've noticed that my voltmeter in the car (auto 86 SE) reads about 15-16 volts when I use the headlights, and 16-17 volts when driving with headlights off. I was afraid that the battery may be overcharging so I checked the voltage of the battery with the car on and off.<P>OFF: 12.6v (normal, right?)<BR>ON:  14.6v (also normal, right?)<P>So does this mean my voltmeter is just a litte messed up, or is it a warning sign of something to come?  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||10-06-1999||06:57 PM||||I wouldn't call myself an expert, but those figures sound normal to me, kornholio.  My 84 was a hair under 13 with the engine off and about 14.7 with the engine on (the gauge in the car read 13.5 and 15).  My 86 V6 is around 12.5 off and 14.5 on (the gauge in the car says 12 off and 14 on).  Trust your multimeter!  Charging system is go, Flight.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||10-06-1999||08:26 PM||||From my past experience,this sounds like a bad diode in the alternator,causing the regulator to sense a low amp situation,and boost things to try and compensate.<BR>The higher voltage may harm things,so better put a more precise meter on there and find out.<BR>Had this happen on an older car with no electronics,and the spark plug wires got fried(no kidding),and it began to cut out at high revs,then lower revs as they got really fried.||||reg||1
Z||000003||kornholio||10-06-1999||10:17 PM||||lowCG, isn't using a digital voltmeter precise enough? The readings I get are normal at the battery, so it's not being overcharged. I'm just wondering why the voltmeter in the car is so high. What does the voltmeter measure anyway? And what would cause it to show a higher voltage than it should?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||10-06-1999||10:26 PM||||My 88 Formula's gage reads high also just at the red portion but I checked it with a very accurate volt meter and I get 14.3 or so. You're not alone.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||10-06-1999||11:46 PM||||Oh,yeah,that's the best way to get to know your car;rechecking with other known instruments,then just knowing that everything is ok with the hardware.<BR>Put a new engine in a car once,only to read the oil pressure as being 25lbs!Put a mechanical guage on it,and it checked out at over 70lbs.Whew.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-07-1999||09:36 AM||||check the ground straps.... those will thro off the dash gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||10-07-1999||10:20 AM||||I dont know the exact specs on the fiero (and mt official service guide is in the mail), but on another vehicle I was supposed to check the voltage at full load (all lights on, etc...) and at a particular RPM then see what the voltage is.  Thinking back now... that one might have had a generator, not an alternator so this info may be useless.  If this is the case, Sorry!||||reg||1
Z||000008||DJRice||10-07-1999||10:51 AM||||If the diode did go bad in your alternator and it has been putting out 16-17 Volts, you need to replace it, and put your jumper cables in the trunk.  I drove around with a bad diode in my alt (in my buick) for about a week and it ended up ruining my less than one year old battery.<P>As always individual results may vary, but It did not take long to kill that battery.||||reg||1
